272
00:14:09,110 --> 00:14:11,110
It's hard to draw the
boundaries between things,

273
00:14:11,110 --> 00:14:13,290
but I think it's
so cool to see how

274
00:14:13,290 --> 00:14:15,330
the boundaries are
kind of breaking down

275
00:14:15,330 --> 00:14:17,363
between these areas.

276
00:14:17,363 --> 00:14:18,780
So yeah, as Emma
said, we're going

277
00:14:18,780 --> 00:14:20,947
to talk a bit
about RLHF and DPO.

278
00:14:20,947 --> 00:14:22,530
And we have a little
bit of background

279
00:14:22,530 --> 00:14:25,890
that I'll do to set
things up for these guys

280
00:14:25,890 --> 00:14:27,203
to bring things home.

281
00:14:27,203 --> 00:14:28,620
And some of this
is probably going

282
00:14:28,620 --> 00:14:30,703
to be review from things
Emma has already covered.

283
00:14:30,703 --> 00:14:33,720
But just to make sure
we're all on the same page,

284
00:14:33,720 --> 00:14:37,740
we are, in fact, talking about
this setting of reinforcement

285
00:14:37,740 --> 00:14:39,850
learning from human feedback.

286
00:14:39,850 --> 00:14:44,970
And as a small piece of
background or setup here,

287
00:14:44,970 --> 00:14:46,980
why are we talking about RLHF?

288
00:14:46,980 --> 00:14:49,200
Why are we doing RL
on language models?

289
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:51,190
Why are we talking about it now?

290
00:14:51,190 --> 00:14:54,510
People did not start doing RL on
language models a few years ago

291
00:14:54,510 --> 00:14:55,690
when ChatGPT came out.

292
00:14:55,690 --> 00:14:58,660
People have been doing RL on
language models for a long time.

293
00:14:58,660 --> 00:15:02,530
But this sort of ChatGPT
moment, so to speak,

294
00:15:02,530 --> 00:15:05,700
is something that I think
really brought these RL methods

295
00:15:05,700 --> 00:15:08,033
to language models into the
forefront of people's minds,

296
00:15:08,033 --> 00:15:10,367
because there was sort of a
sense in which things really

297
00:15:10,367 --> 00:15:12,330
started working for
the first time in a way

298
00:15:12,330 --> 00:15:13,900
that maybe they didn't before.

299
00:15:13,900 --> 00:15:15,900
And a lot of this
comes from being

300
00:15:15,900 --> 00:15:19,110
able to start from a really
strong pre-trained model that

301
00:15:19,110 --> 00:15:22,650
already has a lot of
interesting skills

302
00:15:22,650 --> 00:15:26,230
and pre learned behaviors
that we can fine tune.

303
00:15:26,230 --> 00:15:28,075
And so we don't have
to start from scratch

304
00:15:28,075 --> 00:15:30,450
when we're doing RL, typically,
on these language models.

305
00:15:30,450 --> 00:15:32,408
And that makes it a lot
more kind of accessible

306
00:15:32,408 --> 00:15:35,470
to get some benefit
from these algorithms.

307
00:15:35,470 --> 00:15:36,760
OK, so RLHF.

308
00:15:36,760 --> 00:15:38,530
We have this we have
this three stage

309
00:15:38,530 --> 00:15:41,440
pipeline that is the thing that
has sort of been popularized

310
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:42,710
by ChatGPT.

311
00:15:42,710 --> 00:15:45,640
So in this first stage,
I think Emma actually

312
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:48,290
showed this same figure in
her slide just a minute ago.

313
00:15:48,290 --> 00:15:50,000
So this isn't totally new.

314
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:53,440
In this first stage, there's
really a step 0 here,

315
00:15:53,440 --> 00:15:55,190
which is do the
unsupervised pre-training.

316
00:15:55,190 --> 00:15:56,898
This is when we just
fit a big generative

317
00:15:56,898 --> 00:15:58,370
model of a ton of text.

318
00:15:58,370 --> 00:16:01,780
This is, again, where we learn,
meta learn, in some sense,

319
00:16:01,780 --> 00:16:03,950
some skills we're
going to select from.

320
00:16:03,950 --> 00:16:06,560
And then we're going to
collect some supervised demos,

321
00:16:06,560 --> 00:16:07,910
so from humans.

322
00:16:07,910 --> 00:16:09,460
We'll have some
data set of prompts,

323
00:16:09,460 --> 00:16:11,440
explain the moon landing
to a six-year-old.

324
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:12,880
And a human is
going to write sort

325
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:16,420
of a sensible, good
demonstration response

326
00:16:16,420 --> 00:16:17,300
to this prompt.

327
00:16:17,300 --> 00:16:19,630
And we're just going to do
supervised fine tuning here.

328
00:16:19,630 --> 00:16:22,630
And this is going to actually
serve as that reference policy

329
00:16:22,630 --> 00:16:24,590
that Emma was talking
about a few minutes ago,

330
00:16:24,590 --> 00:16:27,760
the thing that we're going
to constrain our model to,

331
00:16:27,760 --> 00:16:30,340
to make learning
a little easier,

332
00:16:30,340 --> 00:16:32,800
and also, to avoid
over optimization

333
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:35,390
of our approximate
proxy reward function

334
00:16:35,390 --> 00:16:36,582
that we're learning from.

335
00:16:36,582 --> 00:16:39,290
In the second stage, that's when
we do the learning of the reward

336
00:16:39,290 --> 00:16:39,810
model.

337
00:16:39,810 --> 00:16:41,880
So here's when we
collect preference data.

338
00:16:41,880 --> 00:16:43,370
So we're going to
sample responses,

339
00:16:43,370 --> 00:16:46,010
typically, from the supervised,
fine tuned model that we

340
00:16:46,010 --> 00:16:47,160
learned in the first stage.

341
00:16:47,160 --> 00:16:50,090
And we're going to ask a human
or opt in a collection of humans

342
00:16:50,090 --> 00:16:53,330
to provide ranking annotations
over multiple draws

343
00:16:53,330 --> 00:16:55,680
from that supervised,
fine tuned model.

344
00:16:55,680 --> 00:16:57,800
And we're going to use
those preferences to learn

345
00:16:57,800 --> 00:17:01,610
a reward model, so a mapping
from a prompt, a dialogue

346
00:17:01,610 --> 00:17:04,893
history, and a potential
response to a scalar reward.

347
00:17:04,893 --> 00:17:06,310
And then in the
third stage, we're

348
00:17:06,310 --> 00:17:07,140
going to do policy learning.

349
00:17:07,140 --> 00:17:09,800
So we're going to try to fine
tune that supervised, fine tuned

350
00:17:09,800 --> 00:17:13,940
model to generate responses
that receive high reward

351
00:17:13,940 --> 00:17:16,500
from that reward model.

352
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:18,672
OK, so the first step is
pretty straightforward.

353
00:17:18,672 --> 00:17:19,630
Supervised fine tuning.

354
00:17:19,630 --> 00:17:21,270
We don't really need to
talk about it very much.

355
00:17:21,270 --> 00:17:23,560
And again, Emma already
covered some of these things,

356
00:17:23,560 --> 00:17:25,240
so hopefully, this
is mostly review.

357
00:17:25,240 --> 00:17:29,550
But of course, ask questions
if anything seems funny.

358
00:17:29,550 --> 00:17:32,165
Like I said, the feedback here,
the thing we're going to do

359
00:17:32,165 --> 00:17:33,540
is we're going to
get preferences

360
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:34,820
over responses from our model.

361
00:17:34,820 --> 00:17:35,320
OK?

362
00:17:35,320 --> 00:17:38,710
So we're going to end up with
some data set of a prompt.

363
00:17:38,710 --> 00:17:40,560
And this could be
single prompt, or it

364
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:43,020
could be an entire dialogue
history, so multiple turns

365
00:17:43,020 --> 00:17:46,480
and then the most
recent user message.

366
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:49,732
And then this is typically
going to be only two responses

367
00:17:49,732 --> 00:17:51,940
that we're going to do give
a binary preference over.

368
00:17:51,940 --> 00:17:53,710
You can do rankings
over a more responses,

369
00:17:53,710 --> 00:17:58,767
but the returns can be a
plateau relatively quickly.

370
00:17:58,767 --> 00:18:01,350
And it's typically maybe better
to have more prompts and fewer

371
00:18:01,350 --> 00:18:02,797
responses per prompt.

372
00:18:02,797 --> 00:18:04,380
I think it's worth
mentioning briefly,

373
00:18:04,380 --> 00:18:07,530
why are we talking about
preferences over responses,

374
00:18:07,530 --> 00:18:11,880
instead of directly asking
for reward annotations.

375
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:14,460
You could take your
prompt and your response

376
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:19,620
and just ask the human, 1 to 10,
how good of a response is this.

377
00:18:19,620 --> 00:18:21,720
And there are a couple
of reasons for this.

378
00:18:21,720 --> 00:18:24,035
First of all, actually,
in another set of slides,

379
00:18:24,035 --> 00:18:25,410
we have an example
of this, which

380
00:18:25,410 --> 00:18:26,800
I think makes it quite clear.

381
00:18:26,800 --> 00:18:28,467
I don't think we have
them in this deck.

382
00:18:28,467 --> 00:18:32,783
But if you take two
different humans and you say,

383
00:18:32,783 --> 00:18:34,200
here's a prompt
that says write me

384
00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:36,150
a recipe for making
a really good cake,

385
00:18:36,150 --> 00:18:37,748
and you ask two
different humans,

386
00:18:37,748 --> 00:18:39,790
you have two different
responses from your model.

387
00:18:39,790 --> 00:18:41,702
And for human A,
you say, what reward

388
00:18:41,702 --> 00:18:43,660
do you give to this
response and that response,

389
00:18:43,660 --> 00:18:46,170
and another human, you
ask the same question,

390
00:18:46,170 --> 00:18:49,013
you can end up with the
same ranking over responses,

391
00:18:49,013 --> 00:18:50,430
but actually a lot
of disagreement

392
00:18:50,430 --> 00:18:52,812
in the actual rewards
that you're giving.

393
00:18:52,812 --> 00:18:54,270
So people are not
really calibrated

394
00:18:54,270 --> 00:18:56,187
to each other in terms
of the absolute rewards

395
00:18:56,187 --> 00:18:57,930
that they're going
to be assigning.

396
00:18:57,930 --> 00:19:01,620
And it's also just more
cognitively difficult

397
00:19:01,620 --> 00:19:04,710
to assign this absolute
number in contrast

398
00:19:04,710 --> 00:19:08,040
to anchoring to one response,
and then just making a decision

399
00:19:08,040 --> 00:19:10,500
about, is another
thing better or worse.

400
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:13,650
So in some sense, I think
gathering preferences

401
00:19:13,650 --> 00:19:16,230
as opposed to asking humans
to write high quality

402
00:19:16,230 --> 00:19:18,840
demonstrations, or asking
humans to assign directly

403
00:19:18,840 --> 00:19:21,190
the reward itself
is sort of a way

404
00:19:21,190 --> 00:19:26,260
to get higher return of
annotation information per unit

405
00:19:26,260 --> 00:19:29,123
of cognitive effort
of the human labeler.

406
00:19:29,123 --> 00:19:30,790
So we're going to get
these preferences.

407
00:19:30,790 --> 00:19:32,920
And now, we just have
this Bradley Terry model,

408
00:19:32,920 --> 00:19:37,320
which is a very simple model of
discrete choice in humans, which

409
00:19:37,320 --> 00:19:40,150
relates a scoring function,
or in case, a reward function,

410
00:19:40,150 --> 00:19:43,260
so that's this r of
x and a response y

411
00:19:43,260 --> 00:19:46,350
here, to a
probabilistic decision

412
00:19:46,350 --> 00:19:48,930
over two discrete choices.

413
00:19:48,930 --> 00:19:50,880
So here are our
discrete choices.

414
00:19:50,880 --> 00:19:53,232
We have the thing that was
labeled preferred in the data

415
00:19:53,232 --> 00:19:54,690
set, and the thing
that was labeled

416
00:19:54,690 --> 00:19:55,982
this preferred in the data set.

417
00:19:55,982 --> 00:20:00,040
And we wanted to train a model,
some probabilistic model, again,

418
00:20:00,040 --> 00:20:02,970
our reward model, to maximize
the likelihood of this observed

419
00:20:02,970 --> 00:20:03,870
data.

420
00:20:03,870 --> 00:20:06,810
And we need to decide
on some model that

421
00:20:06,810 --> 00:20:09,720
relates a scoring
function to these choices

422
00:20:09,720 --> 00:20:11,230
in order to do
maximum likelihood.

423
00:20:11,230 --> 00:20:14,010
And that is this Bradley Terry
model, which we can then simply

424
00:20:14,010 --> 00:20:18,750
do a maximum likelihood in,
or use this negative log

425
00:20:18,750 --> 00:20:20,310
likelihood of loss.

426
00:20:20,310 --> 00:20:23,490
So we're using this Bradley
Terry conceptual model

427
00:20:23,490 --> 00:20:26,710
of choices, and this turns into
a maximum likelihood of loss.

428
00:20:26,710 --> 00:20:29,720
So we're simply solving a
binary classification problem.

429
00:20:29,720 --> 00:20:31,690
So we have a binary
classifier here,

430
00:20:31,690 --> 00:20:33,550
where the logic
is just our reward

431
00:20:33,550 --> 00:20:35,590
model, the difference
in the reward

432
00:20:35,590 --> 00:20:37,570
we're assigning to
the chosen response

433
00:20:37,570 --> 00:20:41,302
minus the dispreferred
or the rejected response.

434
00:20:41,302 --> 00:20:43,510
We're treating that as a
logit of a binary classifier

435
00:20:43,510 --> 00:20:45,580
and doing maximum likelihood.

436
00:20:45,580 --> 00:20:47,800
So once we do that,
we get a reward model.

437
00:20:47,800 --> 00:20:49,270
We finished step two now.

438
00:20:49,270 --> 00:20:51,100
And now, we need to
just find a policy that

439
00:20:51,100 --> 00:20:53,530
actually optimizes this reward.

440
00:20:53,530 --> 00:20:58,330
And really, this is the
RL bit, so to speak.

441
00:20:58,330 --> 00:21:01,007
And here, we want to
learn, again, pi theta.

442
00:21:01,007 --> 00:21:02,590
This is our policy
that we're actually

443
00:21:02,590 --> 00:21:05,000
fine tuning that we're
actually learning here.

444
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,207
And the objective
here is, we have

445
00:21:07,207 --> 00:21:09,790
prompts where we have some data
set of prompts or conversation

446
00:21:09,790 --> 00:21:13,270
histories and
expectation for responses

447
00:21:13,270 --> 00:21:14,570
sampled from our policy.

448
00:21:14,570 --> 00:21:17,650
We want to achieve high reward,
but that's not the full story

449
00:21:17,650 --> 00:21:19,210
here.

450
00:21:19,210 --> 00:21:24,650
If we just optimize to
maximize the reward here,

451
00:21:24,650 --> 00:21:27,390
what can happen?

452
00:21:27,390 --> 00:21:29,610
I'm not sure if you've
talked about this already.

453
00:21:29,610 --> 00:21:31,040
OK.

454
00:21:31,040 --> 00:21:32,180
Perfect.

455
00:21:32,180 --> 00:21:35,480
Anybody have any worries about
just optimizing this objective,

456
00:21:35,480 --> 00:21:36,930
or are we good?

457
00:21:36,930 --> 00:21:37,770
Because this is OK.

458
00:21:37,770 --> 00:21:39,800
We could tell if you haven't.

459
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:41,570
You can forget the
rest of the objectives.

460
00:21:44,270 --> 00:21:45,788
Perfect.

461
00:21:45,788 --> 00:21:48,080
OK, so one thing that can
happen here is, you remember,

462
00:21:48,080 --> 00:21:50,030
this is not a true
reward function.

463
00:21:50,030 --> 00:21:52,580
This is something we learned
from a finite data set.

464
00:21:52,580 --> 00:21:56,540
And so there's going to be some
distribution, in which this

465
00:21:56,540 --> 00:21:58,410
gives us accurate or
meaningful rewards.

466
00:21:58,410 --> 00:22:00,660
And there's going to be,
outside of that distribution,

467
00:22:00,660 --> 00:22:01,730
there's no guarantee
this thing is going

468
00:22:01,730 --> 00:22:03,080
to generalize meaningfully.

469
00:22:03,080 --> 00:22:05,390
So what we typically
end up doing

470
00:22:05,390 --> 00:22:07,760
is we actually have an
additional constraint, a KL

471
00:22:07,760 --> 00:22:11,420
penalty from our starting model,
the SFT model, or a reference

472
00:22:11,420 --> 00:22:13,612
model to say, I want
you to maximize rewards,

473
00:22:13,612 --> 00:22:16,070
but I don't want you drift too
far from the starting model.

474
00:22:16,070 --> 00:22:18,680
Because again, our
reward model was

475
00:22:18,680 --> 00:22:20,480
trained on preferences
over samples

476
00:22:20,480 --> 00:22:22,010
from that reference model.

477
00:22:22,010 --> 00:22:24,240
So if we drift far from
the reference model,

478
00:22:24,240 --> 00:22:26,360
we're sort of out of
distribution for the data

479
00:22:26,360 --> 00:22:27,920
that our reward
model was trained on.

480
00:22:27,920 --> 00:22:30,440
So we basically can start
getting bogus reward scores

481
00:22:30,440 --> 00:22:35,600
if our policy changes too much
from that reference model.

482
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:36,510
Yeah.

483
00:22:36,510 --> 00:22:38,583
Is the reference
model ever changing?

484
00:22:38,583 --> 00:22:39,750
It depends on the algorithm.

485
00:22:39,750 --> 00:22:43,992
I think in the original
canonical version of RLF,

486
00:22:43,992 --> 00:22:45,450
no, it was a fixed
reference model.

487
00:22:45,450 --> 00:22:46,490
But there have
been a lot of works

488
00:22:46,490 --> 00:22:48,740
since then showing ways to
update the reference model

489
00:22:48,740 --> 00:22:51,452
and use a moving reference
model over time, yeah.

490
00:22:51,452 --> 00:22:52,560
Yeah.

491
00:22:52,560 --> 00:22:54,810
The original data is coming
from that reference model.

492
00:22:54,810 --> 00:22:57,850
You mean that both
those yw and yl,

493
00:22:57,850 --> 00:23:00,160
they are both coming
from the same model?

494
00:23:00,160 --> 00:23:03,220
Again, in the sort of original
canonical form of RLHF, yes.

495
00:23:03,220 --> 00:23:05,530
Since then, people have
proposed a wide variety

496
00:23:05,530 --> 00:23:07,690
of different sampling
schemes, a way

497
00:23:07,690 --> 00:23:10,277
to select what pair
of responses do

498
00:23:10,277 --> 00:23:12,110
you show the human to
get a preference over.

499
00:23:12,110 --> 00:23:14,063
But in the original
vanilla version of RLHF,

500
00:23:14,063 --> 00:23:15,730
yeah, you typically
sample two responses

501
00:23:15,730 --> 00:23:18,490
from the reference model,
get a preference over them,

502
00:23:18,490 --> 00:23:20,150
and use that to learn
your word model.

503
00:23:20,150 --> 00:23:23,242
I've heard that in
practice, but the responses

504
00:23:23,242 --> 00:23:25,450
come from the same model
with different temperatures,

505
00:23:25,450 --> 00:23:26,800
or different models.

506
00:23:26,800 --> 00:23:32,320
Does that, in theory, mean that
we're doing something wrong?

507
00:23:32,320 --> 00:23:35,822
Well, I'm not
sure, in theory, it

508
00:23:35,822 --> 00:23:37,280
means you're doing
something wrong.

509
00:23:37,280 --> 00:23:39,610
I think one way to think
about this is, again, we

510
00:23:39,610 --> 00:23:43,840
want our reward model to perform
well across the state action

511
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:44,620
space.

512
00:23:44,620 --> 00:23:47,860
So if you think of our state
space being, or our context

513
00:23:47,860 --> 00:23:50,200
space being the
conversational history so far,

514
00:23:50,200 --> 00:23:52,580
and our action space
being the response,

515
00:23:52,580 --> 00:23:55,650
you want to have good
coverage over this space

516
00:23:55,650 --> 00:23:58,520
so that you're going to get
meaningful rewards out when you

517
00:23:58,520 --> 00:24:00,000
actually update your policy.

518
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:03,180
And so in principle,
yeah, we would

519
00:24:03,180 --> 00:24:04,680
like to be able to
cover this space,

520
00:24:04,680 --> 00:24:06,470
assuming we have a model
that has high enough capacity

521
00:24:06,470 --> 00:24:07,320
to model all of it.

522
00:24:07,320 --> 00:24:09,528
We'd like to cover as much
of this space as possible.

523
00:24:09,528 --> 00:24:11,600
So yeah, a more diverse
preference data set

524
00:24:11,600 --> 00:24:12,840
is very helpful.

525
00:24:12,840 --> 00:24:14,465
And there's some
trade offs between, we

526
00:24:14,465 --> 00:24:15,882
want to concentrate
our preference

527
00:24:15,882 --> 00:24:17,790
data set on the things
that are high quality,

528
00:24:17,790 --> 00:24:20,760
but also, make sure we
do cover a wide variety

529
00:24:20,760 --> 00:24:25,160
so we don't overestimate
rewards for bad stuff.

530
00:24:25,160 --> 00:24:28,110
OK, one more, and then I'll
hand it off to these guys.

531
00:24:28,110 --> 00:24:30,710
So [INAUDIBLE] learning,
we know that even

532
00:24:30,710 --> 00:24:33,410
if you have limited
data set, but if you

533
00:24:33,410 --> 00:24:37,100
have a large enough
network, then if you train

534
00:24:37,100 --> 00:24:40,140
it to near zero error on
the limited train data set,

535
00:24:40,140 --> 00:24:43,610
it can't generalize well on
the test data set, as well.

536
00:24:43,610 --> 00:24:48,572
Why is that not applicable here,
as well for the reward model?

537
00:24:48,572 --> 00:24:50,030
Well, it is applicable
in the sense

538
00:24:50,030 --> 00:24:53,750
that the same sort of phenomena,
like double descent and things

539
00:24:53,750 --> 00:24:55,800
like this are still
applicable in this case.

540
00:24:55,800 --> 00:24:58,400
So you will get better
performance, typically,

541
00:24:58,400 --> 00:25:00,410
from using a larger
reward model.

542
00:25:00,410 --> 00:25:01,693
But there are limits to this.

543
00:25:01,693 --> 00:25:03,860
There's only a certain
amount of information content

544
00:25:03,860 --> 00:25:06,090
in a finite data
set of preferences.

545
00:25:06,090 --> 00:25:08,690
And so the extent to which
you can push that model

546
00:25:08,690 --> 00:25:10,693
to generalize to new things--

547
00:25:10,693 --> 00:25:12,860
my preference data set only
has questions about what

548
00:25:12,860 --> 00:25:14,350
types of pets someone likes.

549
00:25:14,350 --> 00:25:16,850
It's just not going to tell you
anything about quantum field

550
00:25:16,850 --> 00:25:20,810
theory, no matter how
big you make your model.

551
00:25:20,810 --> 00:25:21,870
So there are limits.

552
00:25:21,870 --> 00:25:25,890
But yes, you would expect
some level of generalization.

553
00:25:25,890 --> 00:25:26,620
OK, cool.

554
00:25:26,620 --> 00:25:28,430
So that is a primer on RLHF.

555
00:25:28,430 --> 00:25:33,060
And, basically, unfortunately,
what we end up with is this--

556
00:25:33,060 --> 00:25:34,650
if we're doing PPO,
for example, this

557
00:25:34,650 --> 00:25:36,480
ends up being really,
really, really complicated

558
00:25:36,480 --> 00:25:37,720
in the policy learning stage.

559
00:25:37,720 --> 00:25:39,150
So there are a lot of
moving pieces here,

560
00:25:39,150 --> 00:25:41,275
and I guess you all will
have the distinct pleasure

561
00:25:41,275 --> 00:25:45,090
of implementing this
for your homework.

562
00:25:45,090 --> 00:25:47,010
Congratulations.

563
00:25:47,010 --> 00:25:48,760
But there are a lot
of moving pieces here,

564
00:25:48,760 --> 00:25:53,130
and that was one of the
motivating reasons for why

565
00:25:53,130 --> 00:25:55,200
DPO came to be,
basically, was that.

566
00:25:55,200 --> 00:25:57,200
PPO, turns out it was
a little bit tricky

567
00:25:57,200 --> 00:25:58,950
to get it to work for
a particular problem

568
00:25:58,950 --> 00:26:02,590
that Rafael was initiating
some research on.

569
00:26:02,590 --> 00:26:05,575
So anyway, that's the
background on RLHF.

570
00:26:05,575 --> 00:26:07,200
And I'm going to
leave it to Archit now

571
00:26:07,200 --> 00:26:10,760
to give an overview of PPO.

572
00:26:10,760 --> 00:26:11,260
All right.

573
00:26:11,260 --> 00:26:12,200
Thanks, Eric.

574
00:26:12,200 --> 00:26:13,540
Is this working?

575
00:26:13,540 --> 00:26:15,030
OK, cool.

576
00:26:15,030 --> 00:26:19,020
All right, who's ready
for the fun math stuff?

577
00:26:19,020 --> 00:26:21,940
So you saw the
scary picture here.

578
00:26:21,940 --> 00:26:24,300
And really, the question
we wanted to start with

579
00:26:24,300 --> 00:26:25,950
is, do we need to
do all this just

580
00:26:25,950 --> 00:26:28,680
to fine tune our model
according to human preferences.

581
00:26:28,680 --> 00:26:30,810
And unsurprisingly,
the answer is

582
00:26:30,810 --> 00:26:34,290
going to be no, so be
prepared for the ride.

583
00:26:34,290 --> 00:26:37,050
And yeah, we saw this
objective earlier.

584
00:26:37,050 --> 00:26:39,963
And before we go
into the math, I

585
00:26:39,963 --> 00:26:41,880
want to just give a high
level picture of what

586
00:26:41,880 --> 00:26:44,040
is going to happen here.

587
00:26:44,040 --> 00:26:46,230
We had some reward function,
which kind of told us

588
00:26:46,230 --> 00:26:48,810
what humans like and
humans do not like.

589
00:26:48,810 --> 00:26:50,790
And right now, we're
parameterizing that

590
00:26:50,790 --> 00:26:53,010
as a separate network,
saying that this will give us

591
00:26:53,010 --> 00:26:55,910
a score for which answer is
good and which answer is bad.

592
00:26:55,910 --> 00:27:00,690
Now, really, can we
leverage the idea

593
00:27:00,690 --> 00:27:03,120
that our language models
have these probabilities

594
00:27:03,120 --> 00:27:04,170
over completions?

595
00:27:04,170 --> 00:27:07,470
And the completions right now
represent any distribution

596
00:27:07,470 --> 00:27:08,260
over the internet.

597
00:27:08,260 --> 00:27:10,840
But can we like overload
it somehow to basically

598
00:27:10,840 --> 00:27:13,510
represent, can we only
put probability on things

599
00:27:13,510 --> 00:27:15,333
that humans like?

600
00:27:15,333 --> 00:27:17,750
And that's roughly the idea
we're going to try to exploit,

601
00:27:17,750 --> 00:27:20,350
is that there's, essentially,
a mapping between the language

602
00:27:20,350 --> 00:27:22,660
model and the
reward model itself,

603
00:27:22,660 --> 00:27:24,520
one-on-one mapping
that you can use

604
00:27:24,520 --> 00:27:27,740
to directly train the policy
on preferences themselves.

605
00:27:27,740 --> 00:27:29,680
And towards the
end of this, what

606
00:27:29,680 --> 00:27:32,590
you're going to have is a
distribution over responses that

607
00:27:32,590 --> 00:27:35,060
are not just arbitrary text
responses on the internet,

608
00:27:35,060 --> 00:27:37,130
but responses that humans like.

609
00:27:37,130 --> 00:27:39,370
And that's where direct
preference optimization

610
00:27:39,370 --> 00:27:40,690
will come in.

611
00:27:40,690 --> 00:27:41,840
How do we do that?

612
00:27:41,840 --> 00:27:43,390
That's where the
math is going to be.

613
00:27:43,390 --> 00:27:45,310
So we saw the RLHF
objective, which

614
00:27:45,310 --> 00:27:47,890
is, essentially, we want to
maximize the expected reward

615
00:27:47,890 --> 00:27:50,890
over completions, and we have a
KL constraint to the reference

616
00:27:50,890 --> 00:27:53,770
distribution.

617
00:27:53,770 --> 00:27:56,467
For now, we're just using
any reward function.

618
00:27:56,467 --> 00:27:58,550
The math is going to hold
for any reward function,

619
00:27:58,550 --> 00:28:00,910
but in general, it's the
learned reward function.

620
00:28:00,910 --> 00:28:03,980
Now, I don't know if this was
covered in the class or not,

621
00:28:03,980 --> 00:28:07,030
but it turns out that this
equation or this problem

622
00:28:07,030 --> 00:28:08,280
has a closed form solution.

623
00:28:10,960 --> 00:28:12,485
OK, great.

624
00:28:12,485 --> 00:28:13,610
I'm not going to derive it.

625
00:28:13,610 --> 00:28:17,060
Maybe I'll leave it as
an exercise for people.

626
00:28:17,060 --> 00:28:19,500
But it's a fun derivation,
and it's not too hard.

627
00:28:19,500 --> 00:28:23,000
So hopefully, you should
find the time to do it.

628
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:26,210
But really, if you've ever
heard of Boltzmann distribution

629
00:28:26,210 --> 00:28:29,240
or something of this form,
this is really just it.

630
00:28:29,240 --> 00:28:31,680
And this is not what
we're contributing.

631
00:28:31,680 --> 00:28:34,355
This is a known result for a
while and it's very intuitive.

632
00:28:34,355 --> 00:28:35,730
It might look
scary for a second,

633
00:28:35,730 --> 00:28:38,000
but really, what it's saying
is that we have the reference

634
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:39,110
distribution that
we started with,

635
00:28:39,110 --> 00:28:40,478
and we had some reward function.

636
00:28:40,478 --> 00:28:42,020
And really, what
we're doing is we're

637
00:28:42,020 --> 00:28:45,200
upgrading the responses by
the exponentiated reward.

638
00:28:45,200 --> 00:28:46,700
So things which
have a higher reward

639
00:28:46,700 --> 00:28:48,560
will have a higher
probability according

640
00:28:48,560 --> 00:28:50,100
to the exponentiated reward.

641
00:28:50,100 --> 00:28:52,515
Now, if you just look
at this is very simple,

642
00:28:52,515 --> 00:28:54,390
but this won't be a
probability distribution.

643
00:28:54,390 --> 00:28:55,848
And the thing on
the left hand side

644
00:28:55,848 --> 00:28:57,210
is a probability distribution.

645
00:28:57,210 --> 00:28:59,790
So we normalize it by
this partition function,

646
00:28:59,790 --> 00:29:01,880
which is the Z of x.

647
00:29:01,880 --> 00:29:05,570
Think of is as summing over
every completion for a given

648
00:29:05,570 --> 00:29:08,100
question x.

649
00:29:08,100 --> 00:29:10,080
Now, you can imagine
that that's a very, very

650
00:29:10,080 --> 00:29:11,340
interactable quantity.

651
00:29:11,340 --> 00:29:13,560
If I start computing
every sentence

652
00:29:13,560 --> 00:29:15,133
and try to measure
the probability,

653
00:29:15,133 --> 00:29:17,050
and then multiply it by
an exponential reward,

654
00:29:17,050 --> 00:29:18,690
that's basically not tractable.

655
00:29:18,690 --> 00:29:25,900
So this equation by
itself is not very useful.

656
00:29:25,900 --> 00:29:27,405
And I went over this.

657
00:29:27,405 --> 00:29:29,780
This is exactly the definition
of the partition function.

658
00:29:29,780 --> 00:29:32,503
We're summing over
every response y.

659
00:29:32,503 --> 00:29:34,420
The pi ref is the
distribution we started with

660
00:29:34,420 --> 00:29:36,640
and the exponentiated
reward, and beta

661
00:29:36,640 --> 00:29:40,180
is the temperature term trading
of the reward and the KL

662
00:29:40,180 --> 00:29:42,490
constraint.

663
00:29:42,490 --> 00:29:44,950
So this is intractable.

664
00:29:44,950 --> 00:29:47,545
We'll hold on to the partition
function for a second

665
00:29:47,545 --> 00:29:48,920
and we'll see what
happens to it.

666
00:29:48,920 --> 00:29:51,280
But really, this result
is a relationship

667
00:29:51,280 --> 00:29:52,780
between pi star
and the reward r.

668
00:29:52,780 --> 00:29:54,700
But now we can do a
little bit of algebra

669
00:29:54,700 --> 00:29:58,210
and shuffle it around and
rewrite the reward in terms

670
00:29:58,210 --> 00:30:01,300
of the optimal policy itself.

671
00:30:01,300 --> 00:30:03,120
So what does this equation say?

672
00:30:03,120 --> 00:30:06,110
We're writing the reward in
terms of the beta log ratio,

673
00:30:06,110 --> 00:30:08,350
where the ratio is between
the optimal policy pi

674
00:30:08,350 --> 00:30:11,360
star and the reference
distribution we started with.

675
00:30:11,360 --> 00:30:13,780
And then there's this pesky
partition function that just

676
00:30:13,780 --> 00:30:16,330
continues to stay on there.

677
00:30:16,330 --> 00:30:18,890
I'm going to try to develop
some intuition here.

678
00:30:18,890 --> 00:30:20,140
This is important.

679
00:30:20,140 --> 00:30:22,570
What it is saying is
that, if an optimal policy

680
00:30:22,570 --> 00:30:24,280
ways puts more
probability distribution

681
00:30:24,280 --> 00:30:26,600
on a response than a
reference distribution,

682
00:30:26,600 --> 00:30:29,110
the reward is higher.

683
00:30:29,110 --> 00:30:30,110
Does that come through?

684
00:30:30,110 --> 00:30:33,200
And if a probability is lower,
then the reward is lower.

685
00:30:33,200 --> 00:30:34,810
And this is intuitively correct.

686
00:30:34,810 --> 00:30:37,220
This is how our reward
function should also be.

687
00:30:37,220 --> 00:30:38,680
If a response is
preferred, then it

688
00:30:38,680 --> 00:30:40,888
should have a higher
probability and a higher reward.

689
00:30:40,888 --> 00:30:42,720
So you can see we are
starting to develop

690
00:30:42,720 --> 00:30:45,220
a relationship between a reward
function and the probability

691
00:30:45,220 --> 00:30:46,125
distribution itself.

692
00:30:48,630 --> 00:30:51,510
Cool.

693
00:30:51,510 --> 00:30:53,887
But the main problem here
is that this is, by itself,

694
00:30:53,887 --> 00:30:56,470
not very practical because the
partition function, as we said,

695
00:30:56,470 --> 00:30:58,140
is just completely intractable.

696
00:30:58,140 --> 00:31:00,720
So maybe let's go
back to what we

697
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:04,320
were doing in the RLHF process.

698
00:31:04,320 --> 00:31:07,477
The high level idea
is that we have

699
00:31:07,477 --> 00:31:09,060
a loss function and
a reward function,

700
00:31:09,060 --> 00:31:10,810
and we're going to use
this transformation.

701
00:31:10,810 --> 00:31:12,160
And once we plug
it all together,

702
00:31:12,160 --> 00:31:13,577
we're going to get
a loss function

703
00:31:13,577 --> 00:31:15,570
on the policies themselves.

704
00:31:15,570 --> 00:31:19,680
And if we go back to our loss
function for the reward bit,

705
00:31:19,680 --> 00:31:21,870
if you remember, the
logit is the difference

706
00:31:21,870 --> 00:31:24,450
between the rewards of
the preferred response

707
00:31:24,450 --> 00:31:26,260
and the dispreferred response.

708
00:31:26,260 --> 00:31:28,780
This is what Eric covered
just a little bit back.

709
00:31:28,780 --> 00:31:30,750
Now, if you look at
this, this difference

710
00:31:30,750 --> 00:31:35,190
is not going to depend on the
input itself or the partition

711
00:31:35,190 --> 00:31:38,650
function itself if we
look at it explicitly.

712
00:31:38,650 --> 00:31:40,740
So this is exactly what
is going to happen here,

713
00:31:40,740 --> 00:31:43,240
is we're going to take the
equation that we took earlier,

714
00:31:43,240 --> 00:31:46,050
express the reward in terms of
the policy we're going to learn,

715
00:31:46,050 --> 00:31:49,800
and we're going to plug it
into the reward modeling loss.

716
00:31:49,800 --> 00:31:53,440
And once you compute
this difference,

717
00:31:53,440 --> 00:31:56,070
this partition function is going
to cancel out because it only

718
00:31:56,070 --> 00:31:58,860
depends on the input
x, and it does not

719
00:31:58,860 --> 00:32:02,770
depend on the output
we're computing it over.

720
00:32:02,770 --> 00:32:08,620
And when we do this, we get our
final beautiful loss function,

721
00:32:08,620 --> 00:32:11,490
which we called the
DPO loss function.

722
00:32:11,490 --> 00:32:14,140
And really, is just a
reward modeling loss.

723
00:32:14,140 --> 00:32:17,410
And let's take a second
to see what it is doing.

724
00:32:17,410 --> 00:32:20,820
What we're trying to do is, we
have a preferred response yw

725
00:32:20,820 --> 00:32:24,660
and a disprefereed response
yl for a given question x.

726
00:32:24,660 --> 00:32:29,430
And we're trying to
maximize this difference.

727
00:32:29,430 --> 00:32:31,720
That's how we would
minimize this loss.

728
00:32:31,720 --> 00:32:33,420
And maximizing this
difference means

729
00:32:33,420 --> 00:32:36,390
that our log probability
on the preferred response

730
00:32:36,390 --> 00:32:38,680
should be higher
than the probability

731
00:32:38,680 --> 00:32:40,730
that the reference
distribution puts on it.

732
00:32:40,730 --> 00:32:43,150
And the log probability
of the preferred response

733
00:32:43,150 --> 00:32:45,190
should be lower
than the probability

734
00:32:45,190 --> 00:32:48,190
that the reference
distribution puts on it.

735
00:32:48,190 --> 00:32:50,350
Does this make
intuitive sense why

736
00:32:50,350 --> 00:32:52,890
this would change the
probabilities in the right way?

737
00:32:57,835 --> 00:33:00,220
Cool.

738
00:33:00,220 --> 00:33:02,500
And yeah, the log partition
function basically just

739
00:33:02,500 --> 00:33:03,180
cancels out.

740
00:33:07,143 --> 00:33:09,310
You can think of this as
being a benefit to the fact

741
00:33:09,310 --> 00:33:11,345
that you can shift the
rewards by a constant.

742
00:33:11,345 --> 00:33:13,220
So often, that's considered
not a good thing.

743
00:33:13,220 --> 00:33:15,845
But here, they're leveraging it
because you can just cancel out

744
00:33:15,845 --> 00:33:16,845
the partition function.

745
00:33:16,845 --> 00:33:18,160
Yeah.

746
00:33:18,160 --> 00:33:18,740
All right.

747
00:33:18,740 --> 00:33:22,150
I'll hand it to Rafael, and
you can go over the results.

