764
00:33:57,410 --> 00:34:00,550
And then, basically, we just
took a bunch of baselines

765
00:34:00,550 --> 00:34:02,110
across that data.

766
00:34:02,110 --> 00:34:04,720
And we fundamentally were
interested in comparing

767
00:34:04,720 --> 00:34:08,469
to what degree is DPO
an actual good optimizer

768
00:34:08,469 --> 00:34:09,500
of the core objective.

769
00:34:09,500 --> 00:34:11,949
Essentially, there's
this reward KL trade

770
00:34:11,949 --> 00:34:14,630
off underlying all of this.

771
00:34:14,630 --> 00:34:19,210
And we basically wanted to
see how good of a Pareto curve

772
00:34:19,210 --> 00:34:23,429
can we extract from that
to see, essentially,

773
00:34:23,429 --> 00:34:27,705
DPO's optimal trade off here
in this simple rule problem.

774
00:34:27,705 --> 00:34:29,370
Define Pareto curve.

775
00:34:29,370 --> 00:34:29,870
I see.

776
00:34:29,870 --> 00:34:30,610
Yeah.

777
00:34:30,610 --> 00:34:34,360
Well, Pareto curve is a general
concept in economics and sort

778
00:34:34,360 --> 00:34:36,070
of decision analysis
and things like that

779
00:34:36,070 --> 00:34:40,100
where we have trade offs
between several things,

780
00:34:40,100 --> 00:34:42,320
for example, in this
case, reward versus KL.

781
00:34:42,320 --> 00:34:44,170
And we're interested
in the optimal trade

782
00:34:44,170 --> 00:34:45,469
off that we can get.

783
00:34:45,469 --> 00:34:46,960
And we say, for
example, one method

784
00:34:46,960 --> 00:34:50,020
Pareto dominates, another
method if, essentially, we

785
00:34:50,020 --> 00:34:53,679
can get something, get
more without giving up

786
00:34:53,679 --> 00:34:54,800
on something else.

787
00:34:54,800 --> 00:34:56,500
So in this case,
for the same KL,

788
00:34:56,500 --> 00:35:01,550
we can get more reward using
DPO than another method.

789
00:35:01,550 --> 00:35:05,110
And we actually played quite
a bit with the baselines here.

790
00:35:05,110 --> 00:35:06,860
I probably spent a
couple of months trying

791
00:35:06,860 --> 00:35:09,080
to push these PPO numbers.

792
00:35:09,080 --> 00:35:12,320
And essentially, it works.

793
00:35:12,320 --> 00:35:14,820
PPO kind of works, and you
get some results there,

794
00:35:14,820 --> 00:35:20,670
but it can't quite catch
up with the DPO objective.

795
00:35:20,670 --> 00:35:22,960
And what I kind of wanted
to include this curve here

796
00:35:22,960 --> 00:35:26,610
in this talk is, essentially,
I think, even now,

797
00:35:26,610 --> 00:35:28,550
basically, almost all
of the RLHF papers

798
00:35:28,550 --> 00:35:33,140
that you read are actually doing
evaluation potentially wrong.

799
00:35:33,140 --> 00:35:35,690
Because you go read these
papers and you kind of

800
00:35:35,690 --> 00:35:38,760
get the win rate, or you get
the comparisons, et cetera.

801
00:35:38,760 --> 00:35:41,030
But none of them really
plot these curves.

802
00:35:41,030 --> 00:35:43,100
For none of them, you
really don't know where

803
00:35:43,100 --> 00:35:45,120
along this trade off you are.

804
00:35:45,120 --> 00:35:47,720
And that number in and
