1009
00:44:31,250 --> 00:44:32,300
model.

1010
00:44:32,300 --> 00:44:37,301
But the next five
are all DPO models.

1011
00:44:37,301 --> 00:44:39,530
And obviously, there's
always more work

1012
00:44:39,530 --> 00:44:41,280
to be done, more
research to be done.

1013
00:44:41,280 --> 00:44:43,700
But in my mind,
this sort of work

1014
00:44:43,700 --> 00:44:46,490
solidified this, that the DPO
implicit reward is about as good

1015
00:44:46,490 --> 00:44:48,560
as the classic RLHF reward.

1016
00:44:48,560 --> 00:44:49,828
We're not losing generality.

1017
00:44:49,828 --> 00:44:51,620
We're not losing
capability for considering

1018
00:44:51,620 --> 00:44:55,190
this implicit model versus an
explicit parameterized one.

1019
00:44:55,190 --> 00:44:56,730
So the other big
question is then,

1020
00:44:56,730 --> 00:44:59,090
does using a weaker
optimizer, such as PPO,

1021
00:44:59,090 --> 00:45:04,040
provide a better solution, gives
you some sort of regularization.

1022
00:45:04,040 --> 00:45:10,310
And basically, started to
look more into this recently.

1023
00:45:10,310 --> 00:45:13,040
Some of the first
feedback we got on DPO

1024
00:45:13,040 --> 00:45:16,350
was, someone tried to train like
a very large scale DPO model.

1025
00:45:16,350 --> 00:45:19,390
And what they said
was, it does well,

1026
00:45:19,390 --> 00:45:21,415
and then it becomes
more and more verbose,

1027
00:45:21,415 --> 00:45:23,040
and then starts
speaking more and more.

1028
00:45:23,040 --> 00:45:24,582
And at some point,
it reaches a point

1029
00:45:24,582 --> 00:45:28,535
where it just won't stop and
just kind of goes off the rails.

1030
00:45:28,535 --> 00:45:30,040
It just can't stop talking.

1031
00:45:30,040 --> 00:45:33,400
And we looked at this on two
data sets, one on summarization,

1032
00:45:33,400 --> 00:45:34,420
one on dialogue.

1033
00:45:34,420 --> 00:45:37,920
And what you can see
here is the distribution

1034
00:45:37,920 --> 00:45:39,880
of lengths of answers.

1035
00:45:39,880 --> 00:45:42,340
And the blue distribution
is the preferred answer,

1036
00:45:42,340 --> 00:45:44,670
and the red distribution
is the dispreferred answer.

1037
00:45:44,670 --> 00:45:48,240
So we can see there's a very
slight bias towards longer

1038
00:45:48,240 --> 00:45:48,760
responses.

1039
00:45:48,760 --> 00:45:50,020
People have biases.

1040
00:45:50,020 --> 00:45:51,700
They prefer more
verbose answers.

1041
00:45:51,700 --> 00:45:55,060
They prefer more like
verbose, longer summaries,

1042
00:45:55,060 --> 00:45:56,250
et cetera, et cetera.

1043
00:45:56,250 --> 00:45:58,830
But once we train with
DPO, under every column

1044
00:45:58,830 --> 00:46:00,870
is a separate level
of regularization.

1045
00:46:00,870 --> 00:46:03,100
Under any level
of regularization,

1046
00:46:03,100 --> 00:46:05,380
this is blown way
out of proportion.

1047
00:46:05,380 --> 00:46:08,730
It's not only DPO is
allocating probability mass

1048
00:46:08,730 --> 00:46:11,100
within the distribution.

1049
00:46:11,100 --> 00:46:14,380
Basically, this green
histogram is the DPO length.

1050
00:46:14,380 --> 00:46:16,450
It's pushing things way
out of distribution.

1051
00:46:16,450 --> 00:46:19,680
And you see, now we have
answers which are significantly

1052
00:46:19,680 --> 00:46:22,740
outside of the distribution
that's covered in our data set.

1053
00:46:22,740 --> 00:46:24,130
So what is happening there?

1054
00:46:24,130 --> 00:46:25,963
And there is this concept
of reward hacking.

1055
00:46:25,963 --> 00:46:27,970
I don't know if you've
covered reward hacking.

1056
00:46:27,970 --> 00:46:33,240
But there's a very famous paper
from OpenAI called Scaling Laws

1057
00:46:33,240 --> 00:46:34,690
for Reward Model Optimization.

1058
00:46:34,690 --> 00:46:36,857
And what they did there is
essentially the sentiment

1059
00:46:36,857 --> 00:46:38,640
experiment, but a larger scale.

1060
00:46:38,640 --> 00:46:40,510
They got some real
human preferences.

1061
00:46:40,510 --> 00:46:43,140
They trained a reward model, a
very good, very strong reward

1062
00:46:43,140 --> 00:46:44,070
model.

1063
00:46:44,070 --> 00:46:45,570
And then they use
that reward model

1064
00:46:45,570 --> 00:46:48,480
to annotate some synthetic
data, synthetic preferences.

1065
00:46:48,480 --> 00:46:50,760
And then they repeated
the whole RLHF process

1066
00:46:50,760 --> 00:46:53,940
on top of the
synthetic preferences.

1067
00:46:53,940 --> 00:46:55,800
And this is what
they discovered.

1068
00:46:55,800 --> 00:46:57,630
So basically, what
this graph is,

1069
00:46:57,630 --> 00:47:00,540
is the same graph I showed
earlier for sentiment,

1070
00:47:00,540 --> 00:47:03,570
except that the x-axis
is a KL constraint,

1071
00:47:03,570 --> 00:47:06,240
and the y-axis is rewards.

1072
00:47:06,240 --> 00:47:08,880
And these things, the
dashed things you see

1073
00:47:08,880 --> 00:47:10,930
are the learned reward
functions in PPO,

1074
00:47:10,930 --> 00:47:14,130
basically, the expected reward
from your model training.

1075
00:47:14,130 --> 00:47:18,210
And the solid lines are the
actual gold reward models.

1076
00:47:18,210 --> 00:47:20,400
So what you're seeing from
a reinforcement learning

1077
00:47:20,400 --> 00:47:22,900
perspective, it looks like the
models are doing really well.

1078
00:47:22,900 --> 00:47:24,970
It's maximizing
reward quite a bit.

1079
00:47:24,970 --> 00:47:28,540
But actually, its quality is
either stagnating or going down.

1080
00:47:28,540 --> 00:47:31,050
And this concept
of reward hacking

1081
00:47:31,050 --> 00:47:33,540
has become quite
prominent since then,

1082
00:47:33,540 --> 00:47:36,568
both for practical purposes,
but for example, the AI safety

1083
00:47:36,568 --> 00:47:38,110
community is very
worried about this,

1084
00:47:38,110 --> 00:47:40,770
the whole like paper clipping
thing, if you've heard about it,

1085
00:47:40,770 --> 00:47:42,978
and the way that, basically,
the model can find a way

1086
00:47:42,978 --> 00:47:44,670
to exploit these
reward functions,

1087
00:47:44,670 --> 00:47:46,560
such that it thinks it's
doing something good

1088
00:47:46,560 --> 00:47:49,320
while it's actually
doing something very bad.

1089
00:47:49,320 --> 00:47:51,760
And basically, these
things are well understood.

1090
00:47:51,760 --> 00:47:53,710
This paper has something
like 200 citations.

1091
00:47:53,710 --> 00:47:56,950
A ton of work has been done
on mitigating these things.

1092
00:47:56,950 --> 00:47:59,948
And the thinking there
is, in classical RLHF,

1093
00:47:59,948 --> 00:48:01,240
I'm learning a reward function.

1094
00:48:01,240 --> 00:48:03,842
I have a proxy reward,
and I'm continuously

1095
00:48:03,842 --> 00:48:06,300
querying that reward with new
data, which might make it out

1096
00:48:06,300 --> 00:48:08,410
of distribution, which
might kick it off course,

1097
00:48:08,410 --> 00:48:09,550
et cetera, et cetera.

1098
00:48:09,550 --> 00:48:12,750
So it's not surprising
that this happens.

1099
00:48:12,750 --> 00:48:15,750
I think by and large, the
community has not realized yet

1100
00:48:15,750 --> 00:48:17,520
that this happens
in direct alignment,

1101
00:48:17,520 --> 00:48:19,958
as well, because, A, there's
no proxy reward function.

1102
00:48:19,958 --> 00:48:22,000
You're directly optimizing
the model on the data.

1103
00:48:22,000 --> 00:48:23,920
And B, there's no new data.

1104
00:48:23,920 --> 00:48:25,720
There's no synthetic
data being sampled.

1105
00:48:25,720 --> 00:48:27,257
It's all within the data set.

1106
00:48:27,257 --> 00:48:29,340
But what we have discovered,
and essentially, this

1107
00:48:29,340 --> 00:48:31,632
is a new result that we are
currently still developing,

1108
00:48:31,632 --> 00:48:33,690
is that, actually,
reward hacking seems

1109
00:48:33,690 --> 00:48:36,660
to be quite prominent
in DPO, and actually,

1110
00:48:36,660 --> 00:48:39,470
all of the DPO variants, things
like IPO and slick, as well,

1111
00:48:39,470 --> 00:48:39,970
do this.

1112
00:48:39,970 --> 00:48:42,180
Have you heard of those?

1113
00:48:42,180 --> 00:48:46,470
And actually, it might even
be more prominent than PPO,

1114
00:48:46,470 --> 00:48:48,940
because PPO is a
weaker optimizer.

1115
00:48:48,940 --> 00:48:52,470
So you have to push
really hard to really hit

1116
00:48:52,470 --> 00:48:54,270
those tail of the
reward function.

1117
00:48:54,270 --> 00:48:57,820
But DPO gives you the exactly
optimal analytical function.

1118
00:48:57,820 --> 00:49:03,900
So in a sense, it sort of
almost hacks in an absolute way.

1119
00:49:03,900 --> 00:49:06,280
So yeah, this is
currently, I think,

1120
00:49:06,280 --> 00:49:09,630
part of the dialogue
and the research

1121
00:49:09,630 --> 00:49:12,550
that the community is not
quite figuring out yet.

1122
00:49:12,550 --> 00:49:14,280
And that's my goal
to put these things

1123
00:49:14,280 --> 00:49:16,690
out that this same reward
hacking phenomena, very

1124
00:49:16,690 --> 00:49:18,430
surprisingly, because
it sort of goes

1125
00:49:18,430 --> 00:49:20,595
against all the intuition
we've had from before,

1126
00:49:20,595 --> 00:49:22,470
happens in these sort
of algorithms, as well.

1127
00:49:27,990 --> 00:49:28,710
Right.

1128
00:49:28,710 --> 00:49:30,800
So it's kind of the
same type of plot

1129
00:49:30,800 --> 00:49:33,150
you see on the left, the
x-axis, the KL divergence.

1130
00:49:33,150 --> 00:49:35,270
And y-axis here is GPT 4
win rate, so basically,

1131
00:49:35,270 --> 00:49:36,800
judgments by GPT 4.

1132
00:49:36,800 --> 00:49:39,710
And each checkpoint, each data
is like a different checkpoint

1133
00:49:39,710 --> 00:49:41,870
evaluated to train with DPO.

1134
00:49:41,870 --> 00:49:43,520
And kind of similar
to before, you

1135
00:49:43,520 --> 00:49:46,490
see that, basically, it's
different model sizes.

1136
00:49:46,490 --> 00:49:49,340
And these are different data,
but what I'm pointing out here

1137
00:49:49,340 --> 00:49:52,250
is the pattern, this
comb shaped pattern.

1138
00:49:52,250 --> 00:49:54,860
You kind of see, the more you
train, sort of like the higher

1139
00:49:54,860 --> 00:49:55,470
KL you go.

1140
00:49:55,470 --> 00:49:56,730
Actually, your performance
doesn't improve.

1141
00:49:56,730 --> 00:49:57,680
It goes down.

1142
00:49:57,680 --> 00:50:00,350
So it's the same reward
hacking phenomenon.

1143
00:50:00,350 --> 00:50:03,110
The theory tells you that
this thing should be monotone.

1144
00:50:03,110 --> 00:50:05,130
You give up some KL,
you get some reward.

1145
00:50:05,130 --> 00:50:06,860
But that's not the case.

1146
00:50:06,860 --> 00:50:08,840
And kind of the
point here is this

1147
00:50:08,840 --> 00:50:10,413
seems to be more
prevalent in this--

1148
00:50:17,390 --> 00:50:18,920
Technically, the
DPO reward function

1149
00:50:18,920 --> 00:50:21,770
is just as good as any
other reward function.

1150
00:50:21,770 --> 00:50:24,450
But if you're
optimizing it too much,

1151
00:50:24,450 --> 00:50:26,490
we might be in this
reward hacking phenomenon.

1152
00:50:26,490 --> 00:50:29,450
And this is where,
potentially, a PPO optimization

1153
00:50:29,450 --> 00:50:32,390
could be more stable or could
be more beneficial because it's

1154
00:50:32,390 --> 00:50:36,420
a weaker optimizer,
essentially, from a [INAUDIBLE].

1155
00:50:36,420 --> 00:50:38,370
So yeah, I think
this is sort of where

1156
00:50:38,370 --> 00:50:41,430
we are with these type
of algorithms right now.

1157
00:50:41,430 --> 00:50:46,140
And I think there's kind of
exciting work to be done again.

1158
00:50:46,140 --> 00:50:50,860
In conclusion, yeah,
we saw of these things.

1159
00:50:50,860 --> 00:50:52,510
But I think it's
