00:53:57,680 --> 00:53:59,660
Yeah, it's kind of
a niche question.

1235
00:53:59,660 --> 00:54:03,520
But I'm kind of wondering, so
what if for a particular prompt,

1236
00:54:03,520 --> 00:54:07,000
all of the samples aren't
that great, but obviously,

1237
00:54:07,000 --> 00:54:09,790
whoever's ranking them
has to rank all of them

1238
00:54:09,790 --> 00:54:12,340
and doesn't have any way of
indicating that even the best

1239
00:54:12,340 --> 00:54:13,875
sample isn't that great?

1240
00:54:13,875 --> 00:54:15,250
I was wondering
if there is a way

1241
00:54:15,250 --> 00:54:19,780
to account for that, any sort of
weighting that could be applied

1242
00:54:19,780 --> 00:54:23,080
to the rankings that would
indicate that the rankings are

1243
00:54:23,080 --> 00:54:26,890
more or less confident overall.

1244
00:54:26,890 --> 00:54:29,800
No, I don't have the means.

1245
00:54:29,800 --> 00:54:32,990
Feel free to interject, but
that's a great question.

1246
00:54:32,990 --> 00:54:34,810
I think general problem around--

1247
00:54:34,810 --> 00:54:37,330
this is almost like the
exploration problem in RL,

1248
00:54:37,330 --> 00:54:42,200
is if you do not like ever
see good trajectories,

1249
00:54:42,200 --> 00:54:44,380
what are you going
to learn without it.

1250
00:54:44,380 --> 00:54:46,250
I don't have any easy
answers, frankly.

1251
00:54:46,250 --> 00:54:48,160
But I think some
things that work

1252
00:54:48,160 --> 00:54:51,010
is, there's other forms
of feedback, as well.

1253
00:54:51,010 --> 00:54:53,153
So this is like
comparative feedback, where

1254
00:54:53,153 --> 00:54:54,320
you're comparing two things.

1255
00:54:54,320 --> 00:54:56,270
But you can give
thumbs up, thumbs down.

1256
00:54:56,270 --> 00:54:58,580
And then if all of
them are just bad,

1257
00:54:58,580 --> 00:55:01,600
you can indicate, optimize
in a different way,

1258
00:55:01,600 --> 00:55:08,350
such that you're down weighting
most of the responses.

1259
00:55:08,350 --> 00:55:11,590
But yeah, this is a good
open problem to look at.

1260
00:55:11,590 --> 00:55:13,540
I think the exclamation
pointed to it,

1261
00:55:13,540 --> 00:55:17,883
in that one thing that people
ask a lot is, how can PPO work.

1262
00:55:17,883 --> 00:55:20,050
Because with PPO, you get
to sample from your policy

1263
00:55:20,050 --> 00:55:22,090
during training so
you can explore,

1264
00:55:22,090 --> 00:55:23,240
and that has to be helpful.

1265
00:55:23,240 --> 00:55:23,740
Right?

1266
00:55:23,740 --> 00:55:26,470
DPO is just from your
fixed preference data set,

1267
00:55:26,470 --> 00:55:28,460
and you're never
sampling during training.

1268
00:55:28,460 --> 00:55:30,850
But I think your question
actually points out the fact

1269
00:55:30,850 --> 00:55:33,070
that, in some sense,
because we have

1270
00:55:33,070 --> 00:55:36,080
this issue of we're optimizing
only a proxy reward,

1271
00:55:36,080 --> 00:55:38,000
we don't get to optimize
the real reward.

1272
00:55:38,000 --> 00:55:39,670
The important
exploration is actually

1273
00:55:39,670 --> 00:55:42,003
the exploration we do when
we gather the data that we're

1274
00:55:42,003 --> 00:55:44,545
getting preferences over that
we're going to learn our reward

1275
00:55:44,545 --> 00:55:45,170
function from.

1276
00:55:45,170 --> 00:55:47,830
Because if we do good
exploration at policy training

1277
00:55:47,830 --> 00:55:50,020
time, but we sample
some great trajectory

1278
00:55:50,020 --> 00:55:52,270
that our reward model doesn't
correctly label as good,

1279
00:55:52,270 --> 00:55:53,507
it doesn't help us.

1280
00:55:53,507 --> 00:55:55,090
So yeah, in that
sense, it's basically

1281
00:55:55,090 --> 00:55:57,468
an exploration problem,
and it's very important.

1282
00:55:57,468 --> 00:55:59,260
That's why I think
these sort of multi-term

1283
00:55:59,260 --> 00:56:03,850
or an iterative process
could be really helpful.

1284
00:56:03,850 --> 00:56:05,100
Let's go to the middle.

1285
00:56:05,100 --> 00:56:05,600
Yeah.

1286
00:56:05,600 --> 00:56:09,220
Do you think a similar
idea could be applied

1287
00:56:09,220 --> 00:56:12,370
for, say, multi-step
kind of reward,

1288
00:56:12,370 --> 00:56:14,560
in which you get a reward
after multiple steps,

1289
00:56:14,560 --> 00:56:18,190
and you have a preference
at the final step?

1290
00:56:18,190 --> 00:56:21,490
But the reward
function was explicitly

1291
00:56:21,490 --> 00:56:27,820
comparing two preferences
between exactly two.

1292
00:56:27,820 --> 00:56:29,140
Can you repeat that?

1293
00:56:29,140 --> 00:56:31,450
I just didn't quite
catch the question.

1294
00:56:31,450 --> 00:56:34,120
I think if you have a multi-step
kind of reasoning process

1295
00:56:34,120 --> 00:56:36,490
and a reward, which
comes at the end of that,

1296
00:56:36,490 --> 00:56:40,570
would this idea apply?

1297
00:56:40,570 --> 00:56:42,530
Yeah, it does work.

1298
00:56:42,530 --> 00:56:46,030
As I said, you can think of
this as a Q-learning problem,

1299
00:56:46,030 --> 00:56:46,970
actually.

1300
00:56:46,970 --> 00:56:50,630
That is, however,
not trivial to show.

1301
00:56:50,630 --> 00:56:51,530
But it does work.

1302
00:56:51,530 --> 00:56:54,080
If you have a problem
where, basically, you

1303
00:56:54,080 --> 00:56:56,190
have a sparse reward
at the end, the model

1304
00:56:56,190 --> 00:56:58,190
does end up doing some
sort of credit assignment

1305
00:56:58,190 --> 00:56:59,490
on the intermediate tokens.

1306
00:56:59,490 --> 00:57:02,192
If you think of this
as a per token MDP,

1307
00:57:02,192 --> 00:57:03,650
you will end up
with something that

1308
00:57:03,650 --> 00:57:07,317
does something interesting
for those intermediate steps.

1309
00:57:07,317 --> 00:57:08,900
It's not doing
explicit bootstrapping,

1310
00:57:08,900 --> 00:57:10,760
obviously, but you do
end up with some sort

1311
00:57:10,760 --> 00:57:12,080
of credit assignment.

1312
00:57:12,080 --> 00:57:15,920
And there are either
several results now

1313
00:57:15,920 --> 00:57:19,620
showing that, if you have
sequence level rewards,

1314
00:57:19,620 --> 00:57:24,890
you can end up doing something
interesting, even though you

1315
00:57:24,890 --> 00:57:28,373
don't have this
intermediate rewards.

1316
00:57:28,373 --> 00:57:29,540
Do you have a question next?

1317
00:57:35,620 --> 00:57:36,190
Yeah.

1318
00:57:36,190 --> 00:57:38,500
Can you go a few
slides back, when you

1319
00:57:38,500 --> 00:57:40,720
talk about the synthetic data?

1320
00:57:40,720 --> 00:57:42,220
Yeah, there.

1321
00:57:42,220 --> 00:57:44,890
Can you just explain,
again, what the difference

1322
00:57:44,890 --> 00:57:46,390
is between the
real and synthetic,

1323
00:57:46,390 --> 00:57:47,800
what they're doing
in both cases?

1324
00:57:47,800 --> 00:57:51,430
Yeah, it's part of the same
sort of sentiment problem

1325
00:57:51,430 --> 00:57:52,940
I was talking about before.

1326
00:57:52,940 --> 00:57:55,550
They had real human data and
train a reward function on this.

1327
00:57:55,550 --> 00:57:58,060
So they want to be able
to measure the real reward

1328
00:57:58,060 --> 00:57:58,750
function.

1329
00:57:58,750 --> 00:58:00,590
So they get this
goal reward model,

1330
00:58:00,590 --> 00:58:03,640
which is trained on this
real human comparisons,

1331
00:58:03,640 --> 00:58:06,040
and they generate data
from their base model,

1332
00:58:06,040 --> 00:58:09,145
and rank that data using
this code reward function.

1333
00:58:09,145 --> 00:58:10,520
So essentially,
they have access.

1334
00:58:10,520 --> 00:58:12,145
They can query the
goal reward function

1335
00:58:12,145 --> 00:58:15,790
and know what the actual score
of these synthetic generations

1336
00:58:15,790 --> 00:58:17,440
is.

1337
00:58:17,440 --> 00:58:20,102
So basically, they can
essentially create these graphs.

1338
00:58:20,102 --> 00:58:22,060
So the reason we're
getting reward hacking here

1339
00:58:22,060 --> 00:58:24,430
is because we're not using
the actual reward function?

1340
00:58:24,430 --> 00:58:27,940
We're using this
synthetic reward function?

1341
00:58:27,940 --> 00:58:30,040
If you train on
any reward function

1342
00:58:30,040 --> 00:58:33,130
with a finite amount
of data, and if you do,

1343
00:58:33,130 --> 00:58:36,170
in the limit of infinite
data, you would probably not

1344
00:58:36,170 --> 00:58:37,080
see this phenomena.

1345
00:58:37,080 --> 00:58:38,872
But because you're
training on finite data,

1346
00:58:38,872 --> 00:58:41,295
there will be errors
outside the distribution

1347
00:58:41,295 --> 00:58:42,170
that it's trained on.

1348
00:58:42,170 --> 00:58:44,450
And some errors would
like skew positive

1349
00:58:44,450 --> 00:58:46,200
or overestimate the reward.

1350
00:58:46,200 --> 00:58:48,530
Some would skew,
underestimate the reward.

1351
00:58:48,530 --> 00:58:51,150
But because you're
optimizing against that,

1352
00:58:51,150 --> 00:58:55,190
you'll end up giving
responses where the errors are

1353
00:58:55,190 --> 00:58:56,660
skewing positive.

1354
00:58:56,660 --> 00:58:59,240
So that's why you start
seeing phenomena where

1355
00:58:59,240 --> 00:59:02,300
your learned reward
is increasing,

1356
00:59:02,300 --> 00:59:05,000
but your true reward
is actually decreasing.

1357
00:59:05,000 --> 00:59:06,390
If you guys think
back to DAGGER,

1358
00:59:06,390 --> 00:59:10,200
where they saw this propagating
herds and supervised learning,

1359
00:59:10,200 --> 00:59:12,210
it's the same thing.

1360
00:59:12,210 --> 00:59:14,460
Here's also another interesting
tidbit of information.

1361
00:59:14,460 --> 00:59:18,290
All these checkpoints,
even the very high KL ones

1362
00:59:18,290 --> 00:59:21,470
that have quite low
success rates, actually

1363
00:59:21,470 --> 00:59:24,860
have very low losses
and very high accuracies

1364
00:59:24,860 --> 00:59:26,810
as reward functions.

1365
00:59:26,810 --> 00:59:29,120
So basically, the quality
of the reward function

1366
00:59:29,120 --> 00:59:32,180
is not necessarily
connected to the performance

1367
00:59:32,180 --> 00:59:35,540
of the downstream policy, which
is quite a surprising result,

1368
00:59:35,540 --> 00:59:37,540
I would say.

1369
00:59:37,540 --> 00:59:39,195
You had a question?

1370
00:59:39,195 --> 00:59:39,695
Yeah.

1371
00:59:39,695 --> 00:59:44,060
So back to the pairwise
comparison you did,

1372
00:59:44,060 --> 00:59:48,320
so if your objects that you are
comparing cannot perfectly do

1373
00:59:48,320 --> 00:59:51,940
this kind of pairwise
comparison, so for example, say,

1374
00:59:51,940 --> 00:59:54,300
the game rock, paper, scissors.

1375
00:59:54,300 --> 00:59:58,690
So rock is bigger, much
preferred against scissors.

1376
00:59:58,690 --> 01:00:01,230
But it's not a perfect
partially ordered

1377
01:00:01,230 --> 01:00:04,230
set, then what can we do?

1378
01:00:04,230 --> 01:00:06,160
If the reward function
is not transitive,

1379
01:00:06,160 --> 01:00:07,980
it's a great question.

1380
01:00:07,980 --> 01:00:11,760
Yeah so there's an interesting
outcropping of work

1381
01:00:11,760 --> 01:00:15,540
that is basically trying
to get away from the reward

1382
01:00:15,540 --> 01:00:20,280
maximization framework and
think of this as a game, where,

1383
01:00:20,280 --> 01:00:23,880
instead of saying I want
to generate responses that

1384
01:00:23,880 --> 01:00:25,720
are the highest
reward responses,

1385
01:00:25,720 --> 01:00:28,330
we should think of this at
the policy optimization level,

1386
01:00:28,330 --> 01:00:31,470
and I should search for a policy
where the average win rate--

1387
01:00:31,470 --> 01:00:32,970
if I take the
expectation of the win

1388
01:00:32,970 --> 01:00:37,980
rate of sampling an action from
the policy that I'm optimizing,

1389
01:00:37,980 --> 01:00:40,960
and then I have some
comparison or adversary policy,

1390
01:00:40,960 --> 01:00:43,720
I'm going to sample an action
from that adversary policy,

1391
01:00:43,720 --> 01:00:46,092
what is the expected
win rate of the action

1392
01:00:46,092 --> 01:00:48,300
sampled from my policy
compared to the action sampled

1393
01:00:48,300 --> 01:00:49,510
from the adversary?

1394
01:00:49,510 --> 01:00:52,870
And so now we have to pick
like an adversary policy

1395
01:00:52,870 --> 01:00:56,230
class, which kind of makes
sense in your rock, paper,

1396
01:00:56,230 --> 01:00:57,115
scissors example.

1397
01:00:57,115 --> 01:00:59,942
Because yeah, there's not like
an optimal action to take here.

1398
01:00:59,942 --> 01:01:01,900
It depends on what the
policy of your adversary

1399
01:01:01,900 --> 01:01:03,692
is here to know what's
good and what's bad.

1400
01:01:03,692 --> 01:01:07,090
So in this case, it exactly
does address this issue of,

1401
01:01:07,090 --> 01:01:09,100
if you have only a
partial ordering,

1402
01:01:09,100 --> 01:01:11,920
you can't necessarily compare
all pairs of responses.

1403
01:01:11,920 --> 01:01:13,640
We can still use
that kind of data.

1404
01:01:13,640 --> 01:01:17,320
We don't have to be
bottlenecked by fitting a reward

1405
01:01:17,320 --> 01:01:18,380
function first.

1406
01:01:18,380 --> 01:01:21,710
So there are methods like a--

1407
01:01:21,710 --> 01:01:22,750
Nash policy?

1408
01:01:22,750 --> 01:01:25,150
Yeah, like direct Nash
optimization, or Nash learning

1409
01:01:25,150 --> 01:01:29,990
from human feedback are
this other kind of newer,

1410
01:01:29,990 --> 01:01:32,235
I guess, family of algorithms
are really interesting.

1411
01:01:32,235 --> 01:01:33,860
Well interestingly
enough, rock, paper,

1412
01:01:33,860 --> 01:01:37,210
scissors doesn't actually have a
deterministic Nash fixed point.

1413
01:01:37,210 --> 01:01:39,070
It has a stochastic
one, but stochastic ones

1414
01:01:39,070 --> 01:01:41,445
are just like equal
probability over everything.

1415
01:01:41,445 --> 01:01:43,570
That's related to some
deeper results that actually

1416
01:01:43,570 --> 01:01:46,090
say examples like this of
plurality of preferences

1417
01:01:46,090 --> 01:01:47,570
are actually unsatisfiable.

1418
01:01:47,570 --> 01:01:49,720
So you cannot actually
theoretically train,

1419
01:01:49,720 --> 01:01:52,680
and in practice, train a model
that will satisfy that set

1420
01:01:52,680 --> 01:01:54,540
of preferences.

1421
01:01:54,540 --> 01:01:55,290
I think it's good.

1422
01:01:55,290 --> 01:01:56,923
That's one way
that is motivated,

1423
01:01:56,923 --> 01:01:59,340
too, is if you have different
distributions of populations

1424
01:01:59,340 --> 01:02:01,410
with different preferences,
even if each of them

1425
01:02:01,410 --> 01:02:04,537
are internally consistent
with transitivity.

1426
01:02:04,537 --> 01:02:05,495
They may not be across.

1427
01:02:08,820 --> 01:02:10,080
Yeah.

1428
01:02:10,080 --> 01:02:16,950
So assuming that reward hacking
is not happening, what in DPO

1429
01:02:16,950 --> 01:02:25,040
prevents it from taking large
steps in the accusation?

1430
01:02:25,040 --> 01:02:26,660
What do you mean by that?

1431
01:02:26,660 --> 01:02:30,320
Assuming we're hacking is not
happening, is there something

1432
01:02:30,320 --> 01:02:34,700
that DPO is doing that's
preventing it from taking

1433
01:02:34,700 --> 01:02:36,610
too large of a step?

1434
01:02:36,610 --> 01:02:39,890
Yeah, I think the
KL regularization,

1435
01:02:39,890 --> 01:02:43,822
if you look at the beta term,
if the beta term is higher,

1436
01:02:43,822 --> 01:02:46,565
the sigmoid essentially
saturates after a point.

1437
01:02:46,565 --> 01:02:47,390
Right?

1438
01:02:47,390 --> 01:02:49,070
And if the beta
term is higher, you

1439
01:02:49,070 --> 01:02:53,550
have to increase the differences
less to satisfy the loss.

1440
01:02:53,550 --> 01:02:57,350
So roughly, the beta
controls how quickly

1441
01:02:57,350 --> 01:02:59,070
you change the loss function.

1442
01:02:59,070 --> 01:03:00,960
But there are other
parameters, as well,

1443
01:03:00,960 --> 01:03:03,990
like learning rates and so on,
which also change affect this.

1444
01:03:03,990 --> 01:03:05,110
So yeah.

1445
01:03:09,760 --> 01:03:12,190
Yeah.

1446
01:03:12,190 --> 01:03:15,280
For this reward
hacking problem, one

1447
01:03:15,280 --> 01:03:17,050
of the methods
that people usually

1448
01:03:17,050 --> 01:03:20,140
try to use to address this
issue is using ensemble models.

1449
01:03:20,140 --> 01:03:20,830
Right?

1450
01:03:20,830 --> 01:03:23,200
Is that something
that could be done

1451
01:03:23,200 --> 01:03:26,830
with direct methods like
PPO, an ensemble of DPOs

1452
01:03:26,830 --> 01:03:29,190
or something like that?

1453
01:03:29,190 --> 01:03:29,690
You could.

1454
01:03:29,690 --> 01:03:31,065
The problem with
that is then you

1455
01:03:31,065 --> 01:03:32,650
have to keep all
the models around.

1456
01:03:32,650 --> 01:03:34,940
But there's smarter
assembling things you can do.

1457
01:03:38,360 --> 01:03:41,930
You don't have to have complete
copies of your entire model

1458
01:03:41,930 --> 01:03:43,320
to have an ensemble,
for example.

1459
01:03:43,320 --> 01:03:46,910
So you can ensemble sub
pieces of your model,

1460
01:03:46,910 --> 01:03:51,110
or even represent your reward
model as a distribution,

1461
01:03:51,110 --> 01:03:52,910
instead of a single scalar.

1462
01:03:52,910 --> 01:03:55,280
And this starts tying back
into these situations where

1463
01:03:55,280 --> 01:03:59,660
we have a variety of
preferences in our data

1464
01:03:59,660 --> 01:04:02,510
that aren't always
consistent with each other.

1465
01:04:02,510 --> 01:04:05,210
One way of modeling this
data better is to say,

1466
01:04:05,210 --> 01:04:07,310
I have a sort of a
non deterministic,

1467
01:04:07,310 --> 01:04:10,940
or I have a multi-modal
reward function instead.

1468
01:04:10,940 --> 01:04:13,160
And if you have a way
of representing this

1469
01:04:13,160 --> 01:04:16,230
with this generative
model architecture,

1470
01:04:16,230 --> 01:04:19,645
then you can still just stick
this into a DPO looking loss.

1471
01:04:25,180 --> 01:04:29,290
Yeah, your answer already
kind of answered my question.

1472
01:04:29,290 --> 01:04:31,600
But I just wanted
to ask, in general,

1473
01:04:31,600 --> 01:04:35,190
what were the promising
directions for addressing

1474
01:04:35,190 --> 01:04:36,215
reward hacking in DPO?

1475
01:04:39,060 --> 01:04:43,140
Well, there's a number of reward
hacking works on classical RLHF.

1476
01:04:43,140 --> 01:04:45,120
There was a huge
number of those.

1477
01:04:45,120 --> 01:04:48,440
Some of those transfer
pretty straightforwardly.

1478
01:04:48,440 --> 01:04:50,550
Here's something I'm
kind of excited about.

1479
01:04:50,550 --> 01:04:53,070
And interestingly enough,
